Think of the SparkSession as the master control room for your Spark application. Introduced in Spark 2.0, it is the single, unified entry point that allows a developer
to interact with Spark's underlying execution engine to read data, write SQL, and manipulate DataFrames.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Before Spark 2.0, the API was highly fragmented. If you wanted to run core RDD operations, you needed a SparkContext.
If you wanted to write SQL, you had to instantiate a separate SQLContext. 
If you wanted to talk to a Hive metastore, you needed a HiveContext. 
The SparkSession cleanly encapsulates all of these into one unified object. 
Under the hood, it still manages the physical SparkContext to negotiate resources with the cluster manager, but it acts as a high-level facade,
giving you direct access to the Catalyst Optimizer, the catalog registry for tables and views, and runtime configuration settings.

Buisness Context :- 
From a business perspective, the SparkSession drastically improved developer productivity and codebase maintainability.
Data engineers no longer have to navigate a confusing web of interconnected contexts just to build a simple ETL pipeline.
It standardized Spark code across the industry, making pipelines faster to write, easier to code-review, and simpler for new hires to understand.

Design Decision :- 

In modern managed platforms like Databricks or Synapse, the SparkSession is already instantiated for you globally as the spark variable.
However, when writing raw production code packaged into CI/CD pipelines,
I always strictly enforce the singleton builder pattern: SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate(). 
This prevents the catastrophic error of a developer accidentally spinning up a second SparkSession within the same JVM,
which can lead to severe memory leaks, conflicting cluster configurations, and completely corrupted temporary SQL views.
